---
/**
 * /crowmark — the flagship product page.
 *
 * ── THE APPROVED DESIGN, AND WHAT WAS TAKEN FROM WHERE ──────────────────────
 *
 * P1 "Obligation ledger", Figma `223:3` on page `Page — Product (CrowMark)`
 * (`223:2`), file wJ9DK6ByFUN6rWe0CpCVPU. P1 was chosen because it is the only
 * variant whose argument survives covering the prose: the award-line bar, the
 * gate and the twelve-month strip each make the point on their own. Its
 * alternating floor/base rhythm is the value separation the platform needs, and
 * that rhythm is spent down the WHOLE page here rather than only over the five
 * designed sections.
 *
 * THE HERO GRAPHIC IS P2's (`223:5`) — three occluding rotated planes, which is
 * the strongest depth answer in the set. P1's flat obligation panel is folded
 * into the front plane, so the three cited figures and the award-evaluation bar
 * that M-P1 animates are both still there. P2's headline is used verbatim
 * ("Find UK tenders. Cite every answer. Prove delivery.") rather than P1's,
 * which is also what keeps the legacy h1 alive for check-content-parity.js.
 *
 * Motion is `Motion — Product page`, `243:2`, frame `243:3`: five keyframe
 * sequences, each with a trigger, a duration, an easing and a reduced-motion
 * state. Every one is implemented below and every divergence from the board is
 * named at the rule that diverges. Nothing is silently reinterpreted.
 *
 * ── WHAT THIS PAGE REUSES RATHER THAN REBUILDS ──────────────────────────────
 *
 * Section.astro (measure, gutter, section padding, the centred head block and
 * its gradient capsule eyebrow) · `.surface` and its lit state · `.sv-raised`
 * for every drawn artefact panel · `.sv-floor` for the alternating step ·
 * `.sv-numeral` for every displayed figure · `.sv-spot` and `.sv-grain` inside
 * the ambient rigs · `[data-field]` for the shared drift, entrance and
 * scroll-driven parallax · `[data-lit]` for the one view trigger · --m-beat /
 * --m-cycle / --m-ease-* for every duration and curve · --c-amber for at risk,
 * --c-orchid for refused, --c-teal for verified.
 *
 * The one thing NOT reused is src/scripts/motion.ts, which owns `[data-lit]` on
 * the homepage. That is a payload decision rather than an architectural one and
 * it is argued in full at the inline trigger at the foot of this file.
 *
 * Not one shadow, radius, colour, timing or card recipe is authored here.
 *
 * ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY GONE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * THE TWO PRODUCT SCREENSHOTS. mark-analytics.png (264 KB) and
 * mark-ppn002-themes.png. specs/architecture/PERFORMANCE-BUDGETS.md measures the
 * six product captures as 58% of the whole build payload, and the standing
 * instruction is purpose-drawn graphics rather than captures because a
 * screenshot on the public site is a recorded credibility defect. The four
 * drawn artefacts below replace them and argue more than they did. No heading,
 * form control or link destination goes with them, so content parity is
 * unaffected; only two <figure>s and their captions.
 *
 * ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY KEPT ───────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * EVERY SECTION THE PORTED PAGE ALREADY CARRIED. The designed spine is added in
 * front of them, not instead of them. A product page that drops the walkthrough,
 * the statute detail, the capability list, the pricing table and the FAQ in
 * order to look designed has traded the thing readers came for against a
 * silhouette, and check-content-parity.js exists precisely to stop a redesign
 * doing that quietly.
 *
 * Every claim keeps its published wording. Two passages explicitly REFUSE a
 * win-likelihood claim, and that refusal is the positioning rather than hedging
 * to be tidied. See the note in data/crowmark.ts.
 *
 * THREE ACCESSIBILITY FIXES FROM THE ORIGINAL PORT STAND: the recommended plan
 * says "Recommended" in text and not only in fill and scale; "Book a demo"
 * announces that it opens a new window; and the screenshot alt that claimed
 * live data is gone with the screenshot itself.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/ui/Button.astro';
import Carousel from '../components/ui/Carousel.astro';
/* A-45. The hero proof card is ONE artefact appearing on two routes, so both the
   drawing and the recipe behind it are shared: the markup is DutyCard.astro, the
   CSS is styles/duty-card.css (loaded by Base.astro), and the figures and
   citations are data/duty.ts. What stays here is the wording, because /crowmark
   addresses the party the duty is imposed on and /crowmark-buyers the party that
   holds it. Until 2026-08-04 the whole recipe was written out in both files and
   every correction had to be made twice. */
import DutyCard from '../components/sections/DutyCard.astro';
import { S52, S71, PPN002 } from '../data/duty';
import { SUPPLIER_SCREENS } from '../data/crowmark-screens';
/* A-88. The PRICED plans, from the file /pricing already publishes from.
   This page's own PLANS (data/crowmark.ts) carries `price: '£49'` as formatted
   display copy, not a number, so building an Offer from it would mean parsing a
   currency string — and a parsed price is a SECOND source of truth for a figure
   data/pricing.ts already owns. Aliased rather than renamed because both arrays
   are legitimately called PLANS in their own file. `astro check` caught the
   first attempt at this, which used the wrong one. */
import { PLANS as PRICED_PLANS } from '../data/pricing';
import { faqPage, breadcrumbs } from '../lib/schema';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
import {
  PROOF,
  KEY_FACTS,
  STEPS,
  BENEFITS,
  STATUTES,
  CAPABILITIES,
  PLANS,
  FAQS,
} from '../data/crowmark';
/*
 * THIS PAGE'S OWN CSS, AND IT IS A FILE RATHER THAN A <style> BLOCK AT THE
 * BOTTOM OF THIS ONE.
 *
 * It was 1,206 lines and 45.7 KB down there until 2026-08-04. Moving it out
 * under ADR 0010 removed the ~5.3 KB of `astro-ivyj52o5` scope classes this
 * page was stamping onto its own elements — /crowmark is the largest document
 * on the site and was 1,147 B inside the 100 KB per-route budget — and put a
 * stylesheet where the other seventeen stylesheets are.
 *
 * IT IS GLOBAL CSS, SO IT IS ANCHORED. Every rule in it descends from
 * `.pg-crowmark`, which layouts/Base.astro puts on <body> from the route. That
 * is not belt and braces: 41 of the 103 class names it selects on also exist on
 * eight other routes, so unanchored it would have restyled them. The file's own
 * header carries the measurement and the specificity argument.
 *
 * The import sits here, last, so this sheet still loads after everything
 * Base.astro imports — the same position the scoped block held.
 */
import '../styles/crowmark.css';

const title = 'CrowMark | Bid and tender software for UK suppliers';
const description =
  'Find UK tenders, draft answers cited to the regulations and your own bids, and evidence delivery after award. From £49/month.';

const CALENDLY = 'https://calendly.com/crowagent-platform/30min';
const REQUEST_ACCESS = '/contact?enquiry=limited-access#contact-form';

const schema = [
  /*
   * SoftwareApplication. Published by the legacy page and dropped by the port
   * (OA-24). It is what makes a product page eligible for a software rich
   * result, and losing it is invisible until rankings move.
   *
   * No aggregateRating and no review: this site does not publish ratings it
   * cannot evidence, and inventing them to win a rich result is exactly the
   * behaviour the product refuses.
   */
  {
    '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
    name: 'CrowMark',
    applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Web',
    url: `${SITE.origin}/crowmark`,
    description,
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CrowAgent', url: `${SITE.origin}/` },
    /* A-88. Without ONE of offers, aggregateRating or review, Google does not
       consider a SoftwareApplication node eligible at all — so this node was
       being ignored outright, silently, while looking complete.
       DERIVED FROM PLANS, exactly as /pricing already does it, so the two pages
       cannot publish different prices for the same product: a hand-written
       price here would be a second source of truth for a number the data file
       already owns. The description above this line already says "From
       £49/month", so nothing new is being claimed.
       NO aggregateRating AND NO review, and that refusal is deliberate rather
       than an omission. It is the standard SEO advice for this exact warning,
       and taking it would mean publishing ratings this site has no evidence
       for — a breach of its own rule that a claim it cannot trace is not made,
       and of Google's review-snippet policy at the same time. */
    offers: PRICED_PLANS.filter((p) => p.monthly !== null).map((p) => ({
      '@type': 'Offer',
      name: p.name,
      price: String(p.monthly),
      priceCurrency: 'GBP',
      url: `${SITE.origin}/pricing`,
    })),
  },
  ...faqPage(FAQS),
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'CrowMark', path: '/crowmark' },
  ]),
];

/** Matches the legacy in-page nav exactly. Four of the sections. */
/* "See it in action" is second because the showcase renders SECOND on the page,
   and a sub-nav whose order disagrees with the page it links into is a small
   lie a reader notices as soon as they use it twice. */
const SUBNAV = [
  { label: 'See it in action', href: '#showcase' },
  { label: 'How it works', href: '#how-it-works' },
  { label: 'Features', href: '#features' },
  { label: 'Pricing', href: '#pricing' },
  { label: 'FAQ', href: '#product-faq' },
];

/*
 * THE HERO ARTEFACT. Three figures, each cited to a named instrument, because
 * the standing rule has two options and no third: a figure is cited to a source
 * or it is visibly marked as an example.
 *
 * s.52 SETS AND PUBLISHES. s.71 ASSESSES AND PUBLISHES. They are two duties and
 * they must never be merged into one sentence — that is the single most common
 * way this page could become wrong, and the whole gap section below exists to
 * draw the twelve months between them.
 *
 * ONLY THE LABELS ARE HERE. The figures and the citations are the instrument and
 * come from data/duty.ts, where they are declared once for both routes; retyping
 * a commencement date into a second file is how OA-29 spread across twelve. The
 * label is the half that is genuinely this page's: a supplier reads "KPIs
 * minimum" as a count in a contract handed to them, while the authority reads
 * the same section as an obligation of its own.
 */
const DUTY = [
  { ...S52, label: 'KPIs minimum' },
  { ...S71, label: 'months to assessment' },
  { ...PPN002, label: 'minimum evaluation weighting' },
];

/**
 * P1's SOLUTION section, `229:2`. Three cards, and the point of the section is
 * that each move leaves something behind rather than that there are three of
 * them.
 */
const MECHANISM = [
  {
    n: '01',
    heading: 'Read the rule that applies',
    body: 'Statute, the tender document and your own past bids are three different sources, and CrowMark keeps them apart. Every retrieved passage keeps its citation from the moment it is read.',
    /* Each card carries its own small drawn artefact. A section whose only
       visual idea is a bordered box with text in it is named as a failure in
       specs/DESIGN-DECISIONS.md, and three of them in a row is that failure
       three times. */
    art: 'sources' as const,
    chips: ['Procurement Act 2023, s.52', 'Tender, Q4.2', 'Your 2024 bid, p.31'],
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    heading: 'Draft the answer, cited',
    body: 'The draft carries the citation inline, so a reviewer can check the sentence against the clause without leaving the page. Nothing is asserted that has no source behind it.',
    art: 'draft' as const,
    chips: ['s.52', 'Tender Q4.2'],
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    heading: 'Hold the promise to the evidence',
    body: 'A priced commitment becomes a KPI in the contract. CrowMark keeps that promise attached to the artefacts that prove it, ready for the twelve month assessment.',
    art: 'link' as const,
    chips: ['Promise: 4 local jobs', 'Evidence: payroll, Q3'],
  },
];

/*
 * CAPABILITY 01's ARTEFACT.
 *
 * THE COLOURS DIVERGE FROM THE FIGMA FRAME, DELIBERATELY. `230:9` draws the two
 * low scores in violet. The palette rule in styles/tokens.css is explicit that
 * orchid and violet mean THE ENGINE REFUSED SOMETHING, and --c-amber, added by
 * owner decision on 2026-08-03, means a fact about the reader's situation that
 * carries risk. A low fit score is the second thing and never the first: it is
 * news about this supplier against this notice, not a figure we declined to
 * print. Drawing it in the refusal colour would weaken the one signal on this
 * site a competitor cannot copy.
 *
 * So the marks follow the product's own three-way verdict, which
 * data/crowmark.ts already publishes — "returns pursue, review or pass with the
 * gaps named" — and every row carries the WORD as well as the colour, so the
 * verdict survives with every pixel of colour removed. WCAG 1.4.1.
 */
const FIT = [
  { name: 'Grounds maintenance, 3 lots', score: 92, verdict: 'Pursue', tone: 'go' },
  { name: 'Managed print, national', score: 78, verdict: 'Pursue', tone: 'go' },
  { name: 'Clinical waste, framework', score: 41, verdict: 'Review', tone: 'risk' },
  { name: 'Highways surfacing, DPS', score: 19, verdict: 'Pass', tone: 'stop' },
];

/*
 * CAPABILITY 03's ARTEFACT — the gate, and it is the section that defines this
 * product against competitors selling success-rate uplift.
 *
 * FOUR FIGURES CLAIMED, THREE TRACED, ONE REFUSED. A version of this artefact
 * that loses the refused row has kept the picture and thrown away the argument.
 * That row may never be cut for space.
 *
 * NO MONEY FIGURE HERE, ON PURPOSE. The homepage's reasoning trace carries
 * "4 jobs x £27,000" with a visible "illustrative" tag, because £27,000 is not
 * a published figure from a named authority and /sources says so in as many
 * words. Rather than repeat a hedged figure on a second page, the rows below
 * are counts and states that need no hedge at all. The panel is still labelled
 * a worked example.
 */
const GATE = [
  { name: 'Local jobs created', src: '4 starts, evidenced', ok: true },
  { name: 'Apprenticeship weeks', src: '52 weeks, recorded', ok: true },
  { name: 'Carbon reduction plan', src: 'published, dated', ok: true },
  { name: 'Local supply chain spend', src: 'no recorded commitment', ok: false },
];

/** Twelve months of delivery evidence, then the s.71 assessment. Decoration:
    the bar heights carry no figure and the strip is aria-hidden. */
const MONTHS = [38, 52, 61, 74, 44, 57, 70, 82, 49, 63, 77, 45];

/*
 * THE SHOWCASE — the eight drawn supplier screens, replacing the four that were
 * here, on the owner's instruction of 2026-08-04.
 *
 * THE SLIDES AND THEIR STRINGS MOVED TO data/crowmark-screens.ts. They used to
 * be an eighty-line array in this file and a matching gap on /crowmark-buyers,
 * which is the shape a rule gets broken in: alt text and captions on this site
 * obey three rules that are easy to break one page at a time, and the sixteen
 * strings that have to obey them now sit together where they can be read
 * against each other. The three rules are written out at the top of that file.
 *
 * WHAT CHANGED BESIDES THE COUNT. The previous set was chosen partly by a
 * constraint that no longer exists: the carousel had one fixed frame, so the
 * four slides had to share one aspect ratio, and mark-fit-score — the best
 * single image on the product — was excluded for being portrait. Carousel.astro
 * now gives every slide its own proportions on a stage that does not move, so
 * the set is chosen by what it argues rather than by what fits. The four
 * desktop screens run in the order the work happens; two tablet screens and two
 * phone screens follow, which is also the answer to "does this work on a
 * phone" without a claim being made about it.
 */
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/crowmark" schema={schema}>
  {/*
    ── HERO ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The head block is Section.astro's: centred eyebrow capsule, h1, standfirst.
    Owner decision 2026-08-02, and it applies to every page.

    DIVERGENCE FROM `223:5`, RECORDED. P2 draws a left-aligned split, copy left
    and planes right. That is a live tension recorded in specs/DESIGN-DECISIONS.md
    section 5 and it is NOT settled, while the centred head block IS a settled
    owner decision. So the composition follows P1, which centres, and the GRAPHIC
    is P2's, which is the half of P2 the brief actually praises. If the owner
    later rules for the split, this hero moves out of Section.astro exactly as
    HeroStack.astro already does on the homepage.
  */}
  <Section
    as="h1"
    eyebrow="Procurement Act 2023 · s.52 · s.71 · PPN 002"
    title="Find UK tenders. Cite every answer. Prove delivery."
    standfirst="CrowMark reads the notice, drafts the answer against the rule that applies, and keeps the promise you priced attached to the evidence that proves it after award."
    id="hero"
  >
    <div class="cw cw--hero" data-spot="off">
      {/*
        The ambient rig, and every value in it comes from the shared layer in
        styles/tokens.css. `data-field="on"` fires the one-beat entrance at LOAD
        rather than on view, because the hero is above the fold at every
        viewport, so asking an observer whether it is visible is a question with
        a known answer and a failure mode. Same reasoning as HeroStack.astro.
      */}
      <div class="amb" data-field="on" aria-hidden="true">
        <b></b><b></b><i class="sv-spot"></i><i class="sv-grain"></i>
      </div>

      <div class="btn-row">
        <Button href={CALENDLY} target="_blank">
          Book a 30-minute call<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new window)</span>
        </Button>
        <Button href="/tools/tender-compliance-matrix" variant="secondary">
          Run the free compliance matrix
        </Button>
      </div>
      <p class="hint">From £49/month. No card needed to run the free tool.</p>

      {/*
        ── THE PLANE STACK, `223:5` ──────────────────────────────────────────
        The drawing is DutyCard.astro and the recipe is styles/duty-card.css;
        both the geometry and the reasoning behind it are documented there,
        beside the declarations they govern. The four strings below are the only
        part of this artefact that belongs to /crowmark rather than to the pair
        of routes, and they are here so an editor can see the supplier's wording
        without opening a component.
      */}
      <DutyCard
        kicker="The duty, one contract"
        rows={DUTY}
        barLabel="Award evaluation, one bid"
        legendNote="the remaining weighting is the authority's to set"
      />

      <ul class="proof" role="list">
        {
          PROOF.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{p.lead}</strong> {p.rest}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <nav class="subnav" aria-label="On this page">
    <ul>
      {SUBNAV.map((s) => <li><a href={s.href}>{s.label}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  {/*
    ── THE GAP, `228:2` ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    THE HEADING DIVERGES FROM THE FRAME AND THE REASON IS A RECORDED DEFECT
    CLASS. P1 draws "Every bid tool stops at submission." That is a categorical
    claim about every competitor and nothing on this site can source it; the
    homepage lifecycle heading was replaced for carrying exactly that class of
    unsourced competitor claim (OA-18). P2's own heading for the same section,
    "The obligation outlives the bid.", makes the identical point about the
    STATUTE rather than about other people's products, and the drawn artefact
    argues it without naming anybody: the bid ends at award, the obligation
    crosses it and runs twelve months further.

    What CrowMark does about that is a claim about our own product, which is
    normal marketing and needs no source.
  */}
  <Section
    eyebrow="The gap"
    title="The obligation outlives the bid."
    standfirst="s.52 puts at least three KPIs into contracts with an estimated value above £5m and requires you to publish them. s.71 makes you assess and publish against those same KPIs at least every twelve months, and on termination. CrowMark works on both sides of that line."
    id="the-gap"
  >
    <div class="cw cw--gap" data-lit>
      {/* A FIGURE: two drawn lanes, a gate rule and a stop scale. The lane
          names and notes are labels pinned to the bars beside them. */}
      <figure class="award sv-raised">
        <p class="eyebrow award__gate">Award</p>

        <div class="award__lane">
          <p class="award__name">Writing the bid</p>
          <span class="award__track" aria-hidden="true"><i class="award__bar award__bar--a"></i></span>
          {/* The span carries the panel colour so the gate rule, which runs the
              full height of the artefact, does not strike through the words. */}
          <p class="award__note"><span class="award__knock">ends here</span></p>
        </div>

        <div class="award__lane award__lane--us">
          <p class="award__name">Your obligation</p>
          <span class="award__track" aria-hidden="true"><i class="award__bar award__bar--b"></i></span>
          <p class="award__note award__note--lit">
            <span class="award__tick" aria-hidden="true"></span>s.71 assessment published
          </p>
        </div>

        <ol class="award__stops">
          <li>Notice</li>
          <li>Bid</li>
          <li>Submit</li>
          <li>Month 12</li>
        </ol>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── THE MECHANISM, `229:2` ───────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <Section
    eyebrow="The mechanism"
    title="Three moves. Each one leaves a record."
    id="mechanism"
  >
    <div class="cw cw--mech" data-spot="off" data-lit>
      <div class="amb" data-field aria-hidden="true">
        <b></b><b></b><i class="sv-spot"></i><i class="sv-grain"></i>
      </div>

      <ol class="mech card-sync">
        {
          MECHANISM.map((m) => (
            <li class="mech__card surface">
              <p class="meta mech__n" aria-hidden="true">{m.n}</p>
              {/* The artefact. Decoration: every word it draws is repeated in
                  the heading and body beneath it, so it is aria-hidden and the
                  card is complete without it.

                  A FIGURE RATHER THAN A PARAGRAPH, and the paragraph was always
                  wrong: this holds a drawn rule and two chips laid out as a
                  picture, not a sentence. `--draft` sets them side by side, so
                  the pair is centred as a pair and neither chip is on the
                  centre line by itself — which is what a drawing does and what
                  scripts/check-render.js correctly declines to ask a <figure>
                  about. */}
              <figure class:list={['mech__art', `mech__art--${m.art}`]} aria-hidden="true">
                {m.art === 'draft' && <span class="mech__rule"></span>}
                {m.chips.map((c) => <span class="mech__chip">{c}</span>)}
              </figure>
              <h3 class="card__h">{m.heading}</h3>
              <p class="card__body">{m.body}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── CAPABILITY 01, `230:2` ───────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <Section id="cap-fit">
    <div class="cw cap cap--art-right" data-spot="off" data-lit>
      <div class="amb" data-field aria-hidden="true">
        <b></b><b></b><i class="sv-spot"></i><i class="sv-grain"></i>
      </div>

      <div class="cap__text">
        <p class="meta cap__n">01</p>
        <h2 class="cap__h">Find the work that actually fits</h2>
        <p class="cap__body">
          CrowMark reads live notices and scores each against what you can evidence today:
          turnover, accreditations, past delivery in the same category. A low score is an answer,
          not a failure, and it comes with the reason. You stop writing bids you cannot support.
        </p>
        <p class="cap__more"><a href="#features">See how scoring works</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="cap__art">
        {/* A FIGURE: scored bars with their names and verdicts set against
            them. */}
        <figure class="fit sv-raised">
          <p class="eyebrow art__kicker">Fit against what you can evidence</p>
          <ul class="fit__rows" role="list">
            {
              FIT.map((f) => (
                <li class:list={['fit__row', `fit__row--${f.tone}`]}>
                  <p class="fit__name">{f.name}</p>
                  <p class="meta fit__verdict">{f.verdict}</p>
                  <p class="fit__score">{f.score}</p>
                  <span class="fit__bar" style={`--w:${f.score}%`} aria-hidden="true" />
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── CAPABILITY 02, `230:28` ──────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <Section id="cap-citation">
    <div class="cw cap cap--art-left" data-lit>
      <div class="cap__text">
        <p class="meta cap__n">02</p>
        <h2 class="cap__h">Every answer carries its citation</h2>
        <p class="cap__body">
          A drafted answer names its source in the sentence: the clause of the Act, the paragraph
          of the tender, the page of your own earlier bid. A reviewer checks it without leaving
          the page, and an evaluator can follow the same trail.
        </p>
        <p class="cap__more"><a href="#benefits">See what sits behind a figure</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="cap__art">
        <figure class="cite sv-raised">
          <blockquote class="cite__q">
            We will hold at least three key performance indicators for this contract and publish
            an assessment against them every twelve months.
          </blockquote>
          <p class="cite__chips">
            <span>Procurement Act 2023, s.52</span>
            <span>Procurement Act 2023, s.71</span>
            <span>Tender, Q4.2</span>
          </p>
          <figcaption class="cite__foot">Three sources, kept apart, named in the sentence.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── CAPABILITY 03, `230:45` — the refusal ────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* ══ THE SHOWCASE ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Owner instruction, 2026-08-03: restore the product showcase carousel that
